1. c7 {Promotion is the only chance of White winning, and therefore this move is
mostly obvious. Remember, if the Black rook ever gets behind the pawn, the game
is drawn. If the King and Pawn are on the same row or file, the Black rook can
come in with a pin, easily drawing the endgame.} (1. Kb7 Rb5+ 2. Kc7 (2. Ka6
Rc5) 2... Rc5) 1... Rd6+ (1... Rd2 $4 2. c8=Q Rb2+ 3. Ka5 Ra2+ 4. Kb4 Rb2+ 5. Ka3
Ra2+ 6. Kb3 Rb2+ 7. Kc3 {And no more checks are available, leading to a
completely winning Queen vs. Rook ending.}) 2. Kb5 $1 {Blocking off squares to the
rook, this move completely and utterly smashes Black into the ground.} (2. Kc5 $4
Rd1 3. c8=Q Rc1+) (2. Kb7 Rd7) 2... Rd5+ {It looks like a perpetual, but even
this series of checks has an end...} 3. Kb4 Rd4+ 4. Kb3 Rd3+ 5. Kc2 {White looks
absolutely winning, but Black has one last trick up their sleeve $1} 5... Rd4 $3 6.
c8=R {But White does too $1 Threatening checkmate, Black's king is in a hopeless
position. As I said earlier, if Black's king were ANYWHERE else, this position
would be likely drawn or even winning.} (6. Kc3 Rd1 7. Kc2 Rd4 {repeating}) (6.
c8=Q Rc4+ {This variation is why White promotes to a rook, because this leads to
stalemate.} 7. Qxc4 (7. Kb3 $4 Rxc8)) 6... Ra4 7. Kb3 {And White wins,
threatening to take the rook on A4 AND checkmate. THIS is the Saavedra
position $1} *